from pathlib import Path
import re,json
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3];D=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
updates={
'journal/small-business-balance-sheet-template/':('Connect a closing balance to period movements','''<p>The balance-sheet workbook records a date, not the profit or cash movement for the month. Use the <a href="/tools/income-statement-template/">income statement worksheet</a> for reviewed period revenue and expenses, and the <a href="/tools/cash-flow-statement-template/">historical cash-flow worksheet</a> to explain changes in cash. They remain separate files and do not transfer values automatically.</p><p>In the new <a href="/journal/small-business-financial-statements/">connected workshop example</a>, opening equity $14,000 plus period net income $3,300 less sole-owner withdrawals $600 gives closing equity $16,700. Closing assets $25,700 equal liabilities $9,000 plus that equity. This is a separate fictional example, not a replacement for the $15,000 workbook example above. Do not subtract withdrawals again when a supplied closing equity balance already includes them.</p>'''),
'journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/':('Compare historical cash with the next forecast','''<p>Keep the historical <a href="/tools/cash-flow-statement-template/">cash-flow statement worksheet</a> separate from this forward-looking forecast. The statement classifies completed operating, investing and financing cash movements; this page assigns expected receipts and payments to future weeks. A receipt already included in reconciled opening cash must not be forecast again.</p><p>Before adding an owner withdrawal, check the lowest dated balance, not only the final week. The <a href="/journal/owner-draw-cash-planning/">owner-draw example</a> shows $2,500 of ending headroom but only $500 above the chosen floor before a collection arrives. An early $2,500 draw would breach that example’s floor. Those figures are a separate scenario from this page’s 13-week table; use the same timing test with your own actual commitments.</p>'''),
'journal/cash-flow-report-template-variance/':('Do not confuse a variance bridge with an activity statement','''<p>This report’s $400 unfavorable closing difference compares a forecast with actual cash. A <a href="/tools/cash-flow-statement-template/">historical cash-flow statement</a> instead classifies actual movement by activity. Both can reconcile while answering different questions; keep their periods, account scope and opening balances explicit.</p><p>For example, a new $2,000 loan received in a period increases actual financing cash but is not customer revenue or improved operating collections. If it was absent from the original forecast, document that funding change as an explanation of cash variance rather than describing it as stronger sales. Use the <a href="/journal/operating-cash-flow-example/">operating-cash-flow bridge</a> to review actual operating generation separately. Do not overwrite the original forecast to remove the evidence of the change.</p>'''),
'journal/financial-dashboard-example/':('Trace dashboard cards to their distinct source reports','''<p>Keep each card’s cost boundary visible when adding formal-report inputs. This page’s $4,000 modeled operating remainder excludes the items stated above; it is not automatically the net income in the <a href="/tools/income-statement-template/">income statement worksheet</a>. That worksheet also separates entered depreciation, other income, interest and tax. Do not relabel a partial operating result as statutory net profit.</p><p>The new <a href="/journal/small-business-financial-statements/">three-report workshop example</a> independently reconciles $3,300 net income, $3,500 operating cash and $3,400 total cash increase. These are separate example figures, not corrections to this dashboard. Use the <a href="/journal/operating-cash-flow-example/">direct/indirect operating cash comparison</a> to explain the difference, and retain source records behind every imported card.</p>'''),
'journal/small-business-bookkeeping-workflow/':('Hand off a reviewed close without losing the evidence trail','''<p>Prepare a close bundle with period and basis, reconciled cash accounts, open invoices, supplier balances, stock evidence where relevant, equipment/depreciation support and owner/loan movements. Assign unresolved classifications to a reviewer before transferring totals into the <a href="/tools/income-statement-template/">income statement</a>, <a href="/tools/cash-flow-statement-template/">historical cash-flow worksheet</a> or <a href="/journal/small-business-balance-sheet-template/">balance-sheet workbook</a>. The tools do not read the ledger or verify those sources.</p><p>For a tangible cutoff check, consider a separate fictional $500 equipment purchase paid at period end. Cash falls by $500 and equipment cost rises by $500 under the stated asset treatment; do not also enter the whole purchase as an operating expense while separately entering depreciation for the same asset. Actual capitalization and expense treatment require your applicable policy. Retain the invoice, payment reference and asset-review decision rather than inferring classification solely from the bank description.</p>'''),
 'tools/service-business-financial-projections/':('Keep projected service results separate from historical reports','''<p>This resource models projected service-business assumptions. The new <a href="/tools/income-statement-template/">historical income statement worksheet</a> accepts reviewed earned revenue and incurred period expenses instead; the <a href="/tools/cash-flow-statement-template/">historical cash-flow statement</a> follows completed movements. None automatically imports this tool’s results or turns assumptions into actual accounts.</p><p>Use the <a href="/tools/startup-budget-template/">startup funding worksheet</a> for a different check: upfront cash uses, a fixed early-trading shortfall and a retained reserve. Its fictional $24,000 target includes $4,000 retained cash, not an additional supplier expense. Enter your actual startup commitments and then inspect payment dates in a <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">dated forecast</a>. Keep a clear label for each scenario, period and basis when comparing the outputs.</p>''')}
log=[]
for owner,(heading,text) in updates.items():
 p=R/owner/'index.html';s=p.read_text();pub=re.findall(r'"datePublished"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"',s);mods=re.findall(r'"dateModified"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"',s)
 s=s.replace('</article>',f'<section id="finance-report-handoff"><h2>{heading}</h2>{text}</section></article>',1) if '</article>'in s else s.replace('</main>',f'<section id="finance-report-handoff" class="tool-panel"><h2>{heading}</h2>{text}</section></main>',1)
 s=re.sub(r'("dateModified"\s*:\s*")[^"]+',r'\g<1>2026-11-18',s);s=re.sub(r'(<meta property="article:modified_time" content=")[^"]+',r'\g<1>2026-11-18',s)
 if 'property="article:modified_time"'not in s:s=s.replace('</head>','<meta property="article:modified_time" content="2026-11-18"></head>',1)
 p.write_text(s);log.append(dict(owner='/'+owner,datePublished=pub,previousDateModified=mods,decision='Substantive report-choice/handoff example and contextual links; original useful content retained',heading=heading))
(D/'audits.json').write_text(json.dumps(log,indent=2));print('Updated 6 existing owners; original publication dates retained')
